- How do you handle re-accesses to recently evicted pages from the remote store to avoid thrashing?

## Why This Is Asked

Beginners should reason about virtual memory protection, per-thread isolation, and fault handling without requiring full OS support. Guard pages are a simple, practical mechanism to detect overflows early.

## Key Concepts

- Virtual memory protection and per-thread stacks
- Guard page placement and page faults
- Graceful thread termination and resource cleanup
- Basic testing for concurrency and fault paths

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal skeleton
typedef struct { void *stack; void *guard; } Thread;

void thread_init(Thread *t) {
  t->stack = mmap(NULL, 8*1024*1024, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
                  MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
  t->guard = (char*)t->stack - PAGE_SIZE;
  mprotect(t->guard, PAGE_SIZE, PROT_NONE);
}

void fault_handler(void *addr, Thread *t) {
  if (addr >= t->guard && addr < (char*)t->guard + PAGE_SIZE) {
    terminate_thread(t);
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to optionally grow the stack on demand?
- What testing strategy ensures no collateral damage when multiple threads fault simultaneously?

## Why This Question Is Asked

Assesses understanding of scalable synchronization across cores and NUMA nodes, combining MVCC, CAS-based publish, and cross-node invalidation. Tests the ability to reason about contention, migration costs, and correctness guarantees in a realistic service-oriented workload.

## Key Concepts

- **MVCC with per-object version counters** enabling concurrent readers without blocking
- **CAS-based publish** and per-object coarse-grained locking for safe writes
- **NUMA-aware traffic routing** and lazy migration strategies to minimize cross-node latency
- **Batched invalidations** to reduce cross-core and cross-node traffic overhead

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal MVCC implementation for acquire/read/commit paths

typedef struct {
    uint64_t version;
    void*    data;
} Object;

// Acquire: snapshot version for consistent read
uint64_t acquire(Object* obj) {
    return obj->version;
}

// Read: fetch data, revalidate version
void* read(Object* obj, uint64_t snap) {
    void* val = obj->data;
    if (__sync_val_compare_and_swap(&obj->version, snap, snap) != snap) {
        return read(obj, acquire(obj)); // retry on concurrent write
    }
    return val;
}

// Commit: atomic swap with incremented version
int commit(Object* obj, void* new_data) {
    Object* new_obj = allocate_staging();
    new_obj->data = new_data;
    new_obj->version = obj->version + 1;
    return __sync_bool_compare_and_swap(&obj, obj, new_obj);
}
```

Under high contention, the retry loop in `read()` converges quickly as CAS failures indicate write-in-flight, and the coarse-grained lock per shard limits serialization to critical sections only, preserving throughput across cores.

## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical understanding of lock-free IPC, memory ordering, and backpressure under contention.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free ring buffer across producers and a consumer
- CAS-based enqueue and acquire/release semantics
- Backpressure strategies and fairness
- Testing under contention

## Code Example

```c
// Producer sketch
bool enqueue(ring_t *r, void *d){
  unsigned int pos = atomic_fetch_add(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (pos - r->head >= r->size) return false;
  r->slots[pos % r->size] = d;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

```c
// Consumer sketch
void *dequeue(ring_t *r){
  unsigned int h = atomic_load(&r->head, memory_order_acquire);
  unsigned int t = atomic_load(&r->tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h == t) return NULL;
  void *d = r->slots[h % r->size];
  atomic_store(&r->head, h+1);
  return d;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to multiple consumers without races?
- How does cache coherence affect performance with large ring sizes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to integrate a simple QoS mechanism with a basic I/O scheduler, showing end-to-end flow from I/O request to wakeup. Demonstrates reasoning about blocking, concurrency, and fairness under bursts.

## Key Concepts

- Token bucket I/O throttling
- Per-process QoS guarantees
- Blocking, wakeup, and race avoidance
- Simple ready-queue scheduling and aging for fairness
- Testability with burst scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal sketch: token bucket I/O
typedef struct { int tokens; int capacity; } bucket;
bucket buckets[MAX_PROCS];

int io_request(int pid, int bytes){
  if (buckets[pid].tokens >= bytes){
    buckets[pid].tokens -= bytes;
    submit_disk(pid, bytes);
    return 1;
  } else {
    enqueue_io(pid, bytes);
    block(pid);
    return 0;
  }
}

void refill_all(int per_tick){
  for (int i = 0; i < MAX_PROCS; i++){
    buckets[i].tokens = min(buckets[i].tokens + per_tick, buckets[i].capacity);
    wakeup_if_waiting(i);
  }
}
```

