## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of IRQ-safe producer-consumer in a tiny kernel.

## Key Concepts

- IRQ-safe synchronization
- Ring buffer wraparound
- Blocking vs non-blocking reads
- Memory barriers and visibility

## Code Example

```javascript
typedef struct {
  uint8_t buf[16];
  volatile uint8_t head;
  volatile uint8_t tail;
} ring_t;

void isr_write(ring_t *r, uint8_t v){
  uint8_t next = (r->head + 1) & 15;
  if (next == r->tail) return; // overflow
  r->buf[r->head] = v;
  __sync_synchronize();
  r->head = next;
  // wake reader if blocked
}

int read_user(ring_t *r, uint8_t *out){
  while (r->head == r->tail) {
    // block or yield
  }
  *out = r->buf[r->tail];
  r->tail = (r->tail + 1) & 15;
  return 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test a bursty ISR and blocked reader?
- How would you generalize to multiple producers/consumers?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design memory accounting and eviction under multi-tenant workloads, a common production pressure point. It evaluates correctness, performance trade-offs, and how to prevent starvation while honoring isolation guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Per-cgroup memory accounting and quotas
- Shared file-backed page cache eviction
- Per-page state and synchronization
- Read-ahead interactions and fairness under contention
- Testing under memory pressure and mixed workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo sketch: decide_eviction_target(cgroup, cache)
function decide_eviction_target(cgroup, cache){
  // choose victim from cgroup's pages with lowest refcount and clean state
  let candidates = cache.pages.filter(p => p.owner === cgroup && p.refcount <= 1 && !p.dirty);
  return candidates.sort((a,b)=> a.lastUsed - b.lastUsed)[0];
}
```

